\title{The One-Dimensional Sperner Lemma as an Exact Signed Count:\\
A Discrete Degree and its Consequences for Fixed Points\\ and Nash Equilibria}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-14}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We refine the one-dimensional Sperner lemma from a statement about parity to an exact integer identity. Given a two-coloring $c$ of the path $0,1,\dots,n$, we distinguish \emph{up-edges} (transitions from $0$ to $1$) from \emph{down-edges} (transitions from $1$ to $0$), and prove the telescoping identity
\[
\Up(c,n) - \Dn(c,n) = \bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)},
\]
where $\Up$ and $\Dn$ count up- and down-edges respectively and $\bval{\cdot}$ interprets \texttt{false} as $0$ and \texttt{true} as $1$. This quantity is a discrete analogue of the \emph{degree} of a boundary map. We show that every classical consequence of the one-dimensional Sperner lemma follows as a corollary of this single identity: the parity form, the balance of ascending and descending crossings when endpoints agree, oriented and unoriented existence of fully colored edges, two discrete intermediate value theorems, and a discrete Brouwer fixed-point statement. We then explain how the discrete fixed-point result is the combinatorial core of Brouwer's theorem and, through Nash's construction, of the existence of mixed-strategy equilibria in finite games. We complement the general theory with a constant-sum criterion under which the uniform strategy profile is a Nash equilibrium, instantiated to a parametric cyclic family of games that recovers Matching Pennies and Rock--Paper--Scissors as special cases.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Sperner's lemma, discrete degree, telescoping sum, intermediate value theorem, Brouwer fixed point, Nash equilibrium, combinatorial topology, game theory.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{MSC:} 05A19, 55M20, 91A05, 54H25.

\section{Introduction}

Sperner's lemma (1928) is a combinatorial statement about proper colorings of triangulated simplices, famous as the discrete counterpart of Brouwer's fixed-point theorem. In its one-dimensional form it concerns a path $0,1,\dots,n$ whose vertices are colored with two colors. The classical statement asserts that if the two endpoints receive different colors, then an odd number of edges are \emph{fully colored}, i.e.\ have endpoints of different colors; in particular at least one such edge exists.

This ``existence via parity'' is enough for many topological applications, but it discards information. Fully colored edges come in two oriented flavors: those that ascend from color $0$ to color $1$, and those that descend from $1$ to $0$. The parity statement is blind to this orientation. Our contribution is to show that the \emph{signed} difference between the number of ascents and the number of descents is not merely determined modulo $2$---it is determined exactly, as an integer, by the colors of the two endpoints.

The resulting identity is elementary but organizing. It plays, in dimension one, the role that the \emph{degree of a map} plays in topology: an integer-valued invariant, additive and conserved, that detects the existence of preimages. From it we recover the full classical package---parity, existence (oriented and unoriented), intermediate value theorems, and a discrete Brouwer fixed point---each as a short corollary. The final link, from the discrete fixed point to Nash's equilibrium existence theorem, is conceptual: we explain the standard reduction (Sperner $\Rightarrow$ Brouwer $\Rightarrow$ Kakutani $\Rightarrow$ Nash) and give a self-contained constant-sum criterion for uniform equilibria, illustrated on a cyclic family of symmetric games.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, a \emph{coloring} of the path on $n+1$ vertices is a function $c : \mathbb{N} \to \{\texttt{false}, \texttt{true}\}$; only the values $c(0),\dots,c(n)$ are relevant. We work over the integers.

\begin{definition}[Color value]
The \emph{value} of a Boolean color is $\bval{\texttt{true}} = 1$ and $\bval{\texttt{false}} = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Up-count]
The number of \emph{up-edges} among the first $n$ edges is
\[
\Up(c,n) = \#\{\, i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\} : c(i) = \texttt{false},\ c(i+1) = \texttt{true} \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Down-count]
The number of \emph{down-edges} among the first $n$ edges is
\[
\Dn(c,n) = \#\{\, i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\} : c(i) = \texttt{true},\ c(i+1) = \texttt{false} \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fully colored edges]
The set of \emph{fully colored edges} is
\[
F(c,n) = \{\, i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\} : c(i) \ne c(i+1) \,\}.
\]
An edge is fully colored precisely when it is either an up-edge or a down-edge, and never both.
\end{definition}

\section{The signed count}

\begin{theorem}[Signed Sperner count]\label{thm:signed}
For every coloring $c$ and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
\[
\Up(c,n) - \Dn(c,n) = \bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rewrite each cardinality as a sum of indicators over $i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$:
\[
\Up(c,n) - \Dn(c,n) = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \Big( \mathbf{1}[c(i)=0,\, c(i+1)=1] - \mathbf{1}[c(i)=1,\, c(i+1)=0] \Big).
\]
A case analysis on the four possible values of $(c(i),c(i+1))$ shows that the summand equals $\bval{c(i+1)} - \bval{c(i)}$ in every case: $+1$ on an up-edge, $-1$ on a down-edge, and $0$ when the endpoints agree. Hence
\[
\Up(c,n) - \Dn(c,n) = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \big( \bval{c(i+1)} - \bval{c(i)} \big),
\]
and the right-hand side telescopes to $\bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)}$.
\end{proof}

The quantity $\Up(c,n) - \Dn(c,n)$ is a \emph{discrete degree}: the net signed number of sign changes, identified with a boundary term.

\begin{proposition}[Splitting]\label{prop:split}
For every coloring $c$ and every $n$, $\ \# F(c,n) = \Up(c,n) + \Dn(c,n).$
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The predicate $c(i) \ne c(i+1)$ is equivalent to the disjunction of the up-edge and down-edge predicates (a four-case truth table). These are mutually exclusive, so the filtered index sets are disjoint and their union has cardinality equal to the sum of cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\section{Combinatorial corollaries}

\begin{corollary}[Parity form]\label{cor:parity}
$\# F(c,n) \equiv 0 \pmod 2$ if $c(0) = c(n)$, and $\# F(c,n) \equiv 1 \pmod 2$ if $c(0) \ne c(n)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Proposition~\ref{prop:split}, $\#F = \Up + \Dn$, which has the same parity as $\Up - \Dn$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:signed}, $\Up - \Dn = \bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)} \in \{-1,0,1\}$, whose parity is $0$ when the endpoints agree and $1$ otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Balanced crossings]
If $c(0) = c(n)$, then $\Up(c,n) = \Dn(c,n)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Equal endpoints give $\bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)} = 0$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:signed} yields $\Up - \Dn = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Oriented existence]\label{cor:oriented}
If $c(0) = \texttt{false}$ and $c(n) = \texttt{true}$, there exists $i < n$ with $c(i) = \texttt{false}$ and $c(i+1) = \texttt{true}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hypotheses give $\bval{c(n)} - \bval{c(0)} = 1$, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:signed} $\Up - \Dn = 1$ and hence $\Up \ge 1$; a nonempty finite set of up-edges contains an element.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unoriented existence]
If $c(0) \ne c(n)$, there exists $i < n$ with $c(i) \ne c(i+1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:parity} the number of fully colored edges is odd, hence positive, so $F(c,n) \ne \emptyset$.
\end{proof}

\section{Discrete intermediate value and fixed-point theorems}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete IVT, upward]\label{thm:ivt}
Let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ satisfy $f(0) \le 0 < f(n)$. Then there exists $i < n$ with $f(i) \le 0$ and $f(i+1) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Define $c(k) = \texttt{true}$ iff $f(k) > 0$. Then $c(0) = \texttt{false}$, $c(n) = \texttt{true}$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:oriented} supplies the crossing.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete IVT, downward]\label{thm:ivt2}
Let $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Z}$ satisfy $f(n) \le 0 < f(0)$. Then there exists $i < n$ with $f(i) > 0$ and $f(i+1) \le 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ivt} with the coloring $c(k) = \texttt{true}$ iff $f(k) \le 0$, which starts \texttt{false} and ends \texttt{true}; its up-crossing is the downward crossing of $f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete Brouwer fixed point]\label{thm:brouwer}
Let $g : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ be a self-map of $\{0,\dots,n\}$ with $g(0) > 0$ and $g(n) \le n$. Then there exists $i < n$ with $i < g(i)$ and $g(i+1) \le i+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Consider the displacement $f(k) = g(k) - k$. Then $f(0) = g(0) > 0$ and $f(n) = g(n) - n \le 0$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:ivt2} yields $i < n$ with $g(i) > i$ and $g(i+1) \le i+1$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:brouwer} is the combinatorial fixed point behind Brouwer's theorem in dimension one: sampling a continuous self-map $h : [0,1] \to [0,1]$ on a grid produces a discrete self-map satisfying the hypotheses, and the located diagonal crossing converges as the mesh tends to $0$, by compactness, to a fixed point $h(x) = x$.

\section{From fixed points to Nash equilibria}

\subsection{The reduction}
A finite game specifies, for each of finitely many players, a finite set of pure strategies and a real payoff depending on the whole profile. A \emph{mixed strategy} is a probability distribution over pure strategies; a profile is a \emph{Nash equilibrium} if no player can strictly increase their expected payoff by unilaterally changing their own mixed strategy. Nash's theorem asserts that every finite game has an equilibrium. The classical proof builds a continuous self-map (or set-valued best-response correspondence) on the compact convex product of probability simplices, whose fixed points are exactly the equilibria; existence follows from Brouwer's (or Kakutani's) theorem. Since Brouwer's theorem is the continuous limit of Theorem~\ref{thm:brouwer}, and that discrete fixed point is a corollary of Theorem~\ref{thm:signed}, the chain
\[
\text{Theorem~\ref{thm:signed}} \Rightarrow \text{Theorem~\ref{thm:brouwer}} \Rightarrow \text{Brouwer} \Rightarrow \text{Kakutani} \Rightarrow \text{Nash}
\]
exhibits equilibrium existence as resting ultimately on a telescoping identity.

\subsection{A constant-sum criterion for uniform equilibria}
Consider a two-player game where player 1 chooses a row $i \in I$ and player 2 a column $j \in J$ ($I, J$ finite, nonempty), with payoffs $A_{ij}$ and $B_{ij}$. The \emph{uniform profile} has each player randomizing uniformly.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform equilibrium under constant sums]\label{thm:uniform}
Suppose there are constants $S_1, S_2$ with $\sum_{j \in J} A_{ij} = S_1$ for every $i$, and $\sum_{i \in I} B_{ij} = S_2$ for every $j$. Then the uniform profile is a Nash equilibrium, with expected payoffs $E_1 = S_1 / |J|$ and $E_2 = S_2 / |I|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If player 2 plays uniformly, player 1's payoff from any pure row $i$ is $\frac{1}{|J|}\sum_j A_{ij} = S_1/|J|$, independent of $i$; hence player 1 is indifferent among all rows and cannot beat $S_1/|J|$, so uniform is a best response. The symmetric argument applies to player 2. Both conditions holding is the equilibrium condition.
\end{proof}

\subsection{A cyclic family of examples}
Fix $n \ge 1$ and identify each player's strategy set with $\Zmod$. A \emph{cyclic game} has player~1's payoff $a(j-i \bmod n)$ and player~2's payoff $b(i-j \bmod n)$ for fixed $a,b : \Zmod \to \mathbb{R}$. Since summing over a full residue system covers each difference once, $\sum_j a(j-i) = \sum_{d} a(d) =: S_1$ and $\sum_i b(i-j) = \sum_d b(d) =: S_2$ are constant. Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} applies.

\begin{corollary}
For every cyclic game the uniform strategy (each move with probability $1/n$) is a Nash equilibrium, with values $S_1/n$ and $S_2/n$. Matching Pennies ($n=2$) and Rock--Paper--Scissors ($n=3$) are special cases.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{Signed count.} Iterate $i$ from $0$ to $n-1$, incrementing an up- or down-counter on each transition; return $(\Up,\Dn)$. Cost $O(n)$; Theorem~\ref{thm:signed} gives an $O(1)$ consistency check.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Discrete root finding.} For $f$ with $f(0) \le 0 < f(n)$, scan for the first positive-turning index (guaranteed by Theorem~\ref{thm:ivt}); $O(\log n)$ evaluations if $f$ is monotone.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Uniform-equilibrium verification.} Check all row sums of $A$ and all column sums of $B$ are constant; if so report the uniform equilibrium. Cost $O(|I|\,|J|)$.

\section{Discussion}
Separating the two orientations of a crossing exposes the degree structure hidden inside the parity statement. This yields uniformity (all classical results become corollaries of one identity), strength (oriented existence is stronger than parity existence), and computability (a free correctness check). Conceptually, the telescoping sum is the discrete boundary operator and the identity is Stokes' theorem in its most elementary incarnation.

\section{Future Directions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Two-dimensional Sperner lemma.} Extend the signed count to an oriented count of fully labeled triangles in a triangulated $2$-simplex via the door/edge-counting argument; the one-dimensional identity is the base-case degree.
\item \textbf{Brouwer in higher dimension.} Package the discrete Brouwer statement as a limit theorem for continuous self-maps of $[0,1]$ using the discrete IVT on a fine grid and compactness, then generalize via the two-dimensional lemma.
\item \textbf{General $2\times 2$ Nash existence.} Prove that every $2\times2$ game has a mixed equilibrium: either a pure equilibrium exists, or the indifference probabilities lie in $[0,1]$, located by the downward discrete IVT.
\item \textbf{Best-response correspondence.} Formalize the best-response map and derive Nash existence from a Kakutani fixed-point theorem whose combinatorial core is the higher-dimensional Sperner lemma.
\item \textbf{Symmetric games.} Show every finite symmetric two-player game has a symmetric equilibrium; the cyclic family supplies test cases.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
We upgraded the one-dimensional Sperner lemma from a parity statement to an exact signed identity---a discrete degree---and showed that the entire classical package, up to a discrete Brouwer fixed point, follows from this one telescoping equation. With a constant-sum criterion for uniform equilibria and a cyclic family of examples, the development traces a clean line from an elementary combinatorial count to equilibrium existence in finite games.

\end{document}

